+**Acceptance response**:
+- `accepted`: boolean
+- `revisionId`: assigned server revision ID on success
+- `conflictStatus`: null if current revision advanced, "competing" if conflict detected
+- `currentRevisionId`: the visit's current resolved revision after this submission
+
+**Rejection response**:
+- `accepted`: false
+- `reason`: enum — "idempotency_mismatch", "invalid_plot", "invalid_data", "unauthenticated"
+- `detail`: human-readable message
+
+## Current State Response
+
+**For a visit with no competing revisions**:
+- `visitId`
+- `currentRevision`: the resolved visit data
+- `revisionId`: current revision identifier
+- `lastModified`: server timestamp of current revision
+- `status`: "resolved"
+
+**For a visit with competing revisions**:
+- `visitId`
+- `currentRevision`: the most recently resolved visit data (may be older than some competing submissions)
+- `revisionId`: current resolved revision identifier
+- `lastModified`: server timestamp of current revision
+- `status`: "needs_review"
+- `competingRevisionIds`: array of competing revision identifiers awaiting resolution
+
+## Resolution Operation
+
+Coordinators resolve competing revisions by selecting one as the new current revision. The operation requires:
+- `visitId`
+- `selectedRevisionId`: which competing revision becomes current
+- `resolvedBy`: coordinator identity (authenticated)
+- `resolutionNote`: optional explanation
+
+The server validates that `selectedRevisionId` is among the pending competitors, advances the visit's current revision to the selected revision with a new monotonic revision ID, clears the competing set, and retains all original submissions with the resolution record.
+
+Coordinators may also choose to create a new revision manually combining elements from competitors, submitted as a new edit with the current revision as its base. This produces the same outcome (new current revision, competing set cleared) but makes the manual combination explicit as a coordinator-authored submission.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency mechanism | Whole-visit revisions with coordinator resolution | Field-aware three-way merge with automatic combination of disjoint changes | Prefer larger review queue over maintaining merge rules that might silently combine scientifically related edits. Workers submit complete visits; coordinators compare whole observations rather than tracking field-level provenance. |
+| Revision ID assignment | Server-assigned monotonic numbers | Client-generated UUIDs, content hashes | Server assignment ensures total ordering and simplifies "current revision" semantics. Clients cannot predict the next revision, preventing race conditions in base revision selection. |
+| Competing revision storage | Accumulate all competitors until resolution | Keep only most recent competing submission per worker | All submissions are preserved per HLD tenet. Coordinators may need to see the sequence of a worker's edits when resolving conflicts, especially when corrections were attempted multiple times. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Should retrieval APIs include full submission history, or only current + competing revisions? Full history may aid debugging but increases response size.
+2. What happens when a coordinator resolves conflicts but new competing submissions arrive while the resolution is in flight? Simplest: coordinator resolution becomes the new base; late arrivals become new competitors.
+3. Should the system auto-resolve when a competing revision is identical to the current revision? Edge case but could reduce coordinator workload.
